      {
        "id": "p20-q85-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Show that $x^2-(2c\\cos A)x+(c^2-a^2)=0$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": {
            "format": "maths-editor-document-v1",
            "version": 1,
            "blocks": [
              {
                "id": "p20-q85-a-short-result",
                "type": "paragraph",
                "fontSize": null,
                "align": "left",
                "spaceBefore": 0,
                "spaceAfter": 2,
                "lineHeight": 1.4,
                "indent": 0,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "x^2-(2c\\cos A)x+(c^2-a^2)=0",
                    "display": false
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "id": "p20-q85-a-short-method",
                "type": "paragraph",
                "fontSize": null,
                "align": "left",
                "spaceBefore": 0,
                "spaceAfter": 2,
                "lineHeight": 1.4,
                "indent": 0,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Apply the cosine rule at ",
                    "marks": []
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "A",
                    "display": false
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": " and rearrange.",
                    "marks": []
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          "worked": "By the cosine rule at $A$,\n$$\\begin{align*}a^2&=x^2+c^2-2cx\\cos A\\\\x^2-2cx\\cos A+c^2-a^2&=0\\\\x^2-(2c\\cos A)x+(c^2-a^2)&=0.\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 36
      },
      {
        "id": "p20-q85-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "If the equation has two distinct positive roots, show that\n$$c\\sin A<a<c.$$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": {
            "format": "maths-editor-document-v1",
            "version": 1,
            "blocks": [
              {
                "id": "p20-q85-b-short-result",
                "type": "paragraph",
                "fontSize": null,
                "align": "left",
                "spaceBefore": 0,
                "spaceAfter": 2,
                "lineHeight": 1.4,
                "indent": 0,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "c\\sin A<a<c",
                    "display": false
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "id": "p20-q85-b-short-method",
                "type": "paragraph",
                "fontSize": null,
                "align": "left",
                "spaceBefore": 0,
                "spaceAfter": 2,
                "lineHeight": 1.4,
                "indent": 0,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Distinct real roots give ",
                    "marks": []
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "\\Delta=4(a^2-c^2\\sin^2 A)>0",
                    "display": false
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "; positive roots give ",
                    "marks": []
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "c^2-a^2>0",
                    "display": false
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": ".",
                    "marks": []
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          "worked": "Let the two distinct positive roots be $x_1$ and $x_2$. The discriminant is\n$$\\begin{align*}\\Delta&=(-2c\\cos A)^2-4(c^2-a^2)\\\\\\Delta&=4c^2\\cos^2 A-4c^2+4a^2\\\\\\Delta&=4(a^2-c^2\\sin^2 A).\\end{align*}$$\nSince $\\Delta>0$ and a triangle has $0<A<180^\\circ$, with $a,c>0$,\n$$\\begin{align*}a^2&>c^2\\sin^2 A\\\\a&>c\\sin A.\\end{align*}$$\nBoth roots are positive, so their product is positive:\n$$\\begin{align*}x_1x_2&=c^2-a^2>0\\\\a^2&<c^2.\\end{align*}$$\nThus $a<c$, and combining the bounds gives\n$$c\\sin A<a<c.$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 100
      }
    ]
